    // THE PERIMETER — generated, not hand-typed, by walking every non-floor cell orthogonally
    // adjacent to floor, PLUS every cell that touches floor only DIAGONALLY
    // (`scripts/gen_site_perimeter.py`).
    // Hand-typing ~220 entries is how a gap gets left in a wall that nobody notices for a month.
    //
    // The diagonal half is each room's convex (outer) corner — 16 cells, one per outside corner —
    // and it was missing until 2026-08-01, which left an open notch you could see straight through at
    // each. Found by screenshotting the perimeter, not by a test.
    //
    // What this list is FOR, as of the 2026-08-01 rewrite: it states which cells are wall, which is
    // what `is_walkable` and `SiteLayout::validate` read. It no longer says where a wall FACE is.
    // `site::visuals::wall_panels` derives that from FLOOR EDGES — one 1 m panel per floor-cell edge
    // whose neighbour is a wall cell — and `corner_vertices` caps the lattice points where two
    // perpendicular panels meet (28 of them). Two consequences worth knowing before editing below:
    //
    //   * `yaw` on a `Wall` row is not read by the spawner any more. It is retained because it still
    //     documents the run's direction for a human, and `gen_site_perimeter.py` still emits it.
    //   * The 16 diagonal-only cells emit no panel of their own (they share no EDGE with any floor
    //     cell, only a corner). They are still the truth about which cells are wall, and their
    //     corner is capped by the two runs that meet there.
    //
    // Non-`Wall` pieces on this list — a column standing in a run, say — are still placed at their
    // cell centre with their authored yaw: those are furniture on a cell, not a face on an edge.
    //
    // Safe by construction: `is_walkable = is_floor && !wall`, and every cell below is NOT floor, so
    // no wall here can change walkability or break `SiteLayout::validate`'s connectivity flood-fill.
    // The two cells the ASYNC aperture occupies (x=6..7, z=1) are held clear so the doorway the
    // spawner places is not bricked up behind a wall. TWO, not four: `doorframe_double.glb` spans
    // 2.003 m, so centred on the gap it covers exactly these two. Holding x=5..8 clear — as this did
    // until 2026-08-01 — left a metre of open perimeter either side of the frame with nothing in it.
    // `SiteLayout::validate` now checks the frame's span against the gap, so the two cannot drift.
    //
    // Openings are the ABSENCE of wall: the wings meet the spine along z=12/z=14 with nothing between
    // them, which is why the flood-fill still reaches every area.
    //
    // Yaw is degrees about +Y. The Ozea wall is 0.10 m thin along X and 1.00 m long along Z, so a wall
    // separating along X takes yaw 0 and one separating along Z takes yaw 90.
    walls: [
        ( piece: Wall,        cell: (  1,  1), yaw:   0.0 ),
        ( piece: Wall,        cell: (  1,  2), yaw:   0.0 ),
        ( piece: Wall,        cell: (  1,  3), yaw:   0.0 ),
        ( piece: Wall,        cell: (  1,  4), yaw:   0.0 ),
